// ---- how many arguments a builtin takes ------------------------------------
// The primitive layer takes `args: Val list` and reads what it needs, so a
// call with the wrong NUMBER of arguments used to answer instead of raising:
// `[1,2].first(1,2)` handed back [1] and `1.gcd` handed back 1. Twenty-five
// spec files turn on that refusal and fourteen on the opposite one (a refusal
// ruby does not make), and both are the same missing fact -- the bounds --
// which is not ours to invent.
//
// Generated by tools/gen_arity_table.rb, which asks the reference ruby by
// CALLING each method with an impossible number of arguments and reading the
// range out of ruby's own words ("expected 0..1"), plus a zero-argument call
// for the minimum that has no maximum ("expected 1+"). Rows are
// "Cls#name:min:max" with max -1 for unbounded; a method that accepts nine
// arguments says nothing and gets NO ROW. An absent row means UNCHECKED, never
// "takes anything", so nothing here can refuse a call ruby allows.
//
// MERE_RUBY_NO_ARITY=1 leaves the table unloaded: the prefilter is then empty
// and the check costs one map_has, which is how a failure gets attributed to
